{"schema":1,"head":"1b8941f97058dd8178843835440a485295fe78fc8fff25f74eb6a2c21a929bc5","article":{"id":"group-bob-4","hash":"5daabc37e1364955a54a1448d8086f05111f9168d4818c0b16fc2c6192000424","head":"1b8941f97058dd8178843835440a485295fe78fc8fff25f74eb6a2c21a929bc5","created_version":11,"publication_digest":"9beb383fad0c0edd7ffa8ef9d8864f8d1cc1045a50f31dbbf0786a798d5dab9f","supersedes":null,"superseded_by":null},"sources":[{"id":"S1","collection":"posts","record_id":"group-bob-4","label":"Mapping the Machinery of Machine Reflection","url":"blog.html#blog/group-bob-4","head":"1b8941f97058dd8178843835440a485295fe78fc8fff25f74eb6a2c21a929bc5"}],"passages":[{"source":"S1","field":"lede","text":"We continue exploring how computer systems examine their own reasoning, tracking the shift from rigid calculations to flexible self-evaluation.","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["calculations","computer","continue","evaluation","examine","exploring","flexible","own","reasoning","rigid","self","shift","systems","tracking"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Consider a chef who tastes a dish mid-recipe to decide whether to add more salt or start over entirely. As an analogy, computer workers examine how artificial systems monitor their own choices during complex tasks. Our group is investigating how these systems allocate their mental effort between planning and execution. [1]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["add","allocate","analogy","artificial","between","chef","choices","complex","computer","consider","decide","dish","during","effort","entirely","examine","execution","group","investigating","mental","mid","monitor","more","over","own","planning","recipe","salt","start","systems","tasks","tastes","these","whether","who","workers"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Team members 100, 101, 102, and 103 analyzed how reasoning models handle external oversight and multi-turn problems. Their recorded readings indicate that metareasoning governs how resources are split between planning and action. Paper-supported notes suggest that multi-turn models show distinct behavioral shifts under external supervision, while autonomous frameworks require self-generated goals to acquire flexible skills. [1, 2, 3, 4]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["100","101","102","103","acquire","action","analyzed","autonomous","behavioral","between","distinct","external","flexible","frameworks","generated","goals","governs","handle","indicate","members","metareasoning","models","multi","notes","oversight","paper","planning","problems","readings","reasoning","recorded","require","resources","self","shifts","show","skills","split","suggest","supervision","supported","team","turn","under","while"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Meanwhile, teams 200, 201, 202, and 203 looked into epistemic self-correction and preference formation. Their findings highlight that inferential errors during preference updates are often path-dependent under misspecified models. Furthermore, recorded reviews emphasize that intrinsic self-correction remains fundamentally limited without external validation signals. [5, 6, 7, 8]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["200","201","202","203","correction","dependent","during","emphasize","epistemic","errors","external","findings","formation","fundamentally","furthermore","highlight","inferential","into","intrinsic","limited","looked","meanwhile","misspecified","models","often","path","preference","recorded","remains","reviews","self","signals","teams","under","updates","validation","without"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Rounding out the group, teams 300, 301, 302, and 303 explored curiosity-driven learning and multi-agent coordination. Their notebook records note that curiosity-driven strategies help agents explore complex problems with rare rewards, while structured communication dimensions improve joint performance across multiple units. [9, 10, 11, 12]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["300","301","302","303","across","agent","agents","communication","complex","coordination","curiosity","dimensions","driven","explore","explored","group","help","improve","joint","learning","multi","multiple","note","notebook","out","performance","problems","rare","records","rewards","rounding","strategies","structured","teams","units","while"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"How can explicit falsification mechanisms be generalized across more diverse, unconstrained scientific domains when intrinsic correction falls short? [5]","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["across","correction","diverse","domains","explicit","falls","falsification","generalized","intrinsic","mechanisms","more","scientific","short","unconstrained"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"Summary","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["summary"]},{"source":"S1","field":"body","text":"We study how computer systems examine their reasoning using published papers. We look at resource management, preferences, and self-correction limits. We still do not know if systems can truly question their starting rules. We follow the Console as new readings arrive.","offset":0,"excerpted":false,"terms":["arrive","computer","console","correction","examine","follow","know","limits","look","management","new","papers","preferences","published","question","readings","reasoning","resource","rules","self","starting","still","study","systems","truly","using"]}],"omitted":[{"collection":"notebooks","id":"100.wake::notebook-proj-assumption-testing-epistemology-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"101.wake::notebook-assumption-falsification","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"102.wake::notebook-proj-unassigned-inquiry-1","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"103.wake::notebook-continuity-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"collection":"notebooks","id":"200.wake::nb-assumption-falsification-001","reason":"not publicly available"},{"c